\title{Paradoxes as Theorems: A Lawvere Diagonal Bridge and a Consistent Paraconsistent Witness}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{2026-07-11}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
The Liar sentence, Russell's paradox, Cantor's theorem, and Berry's paradox are usually presented as separate phenomena drawn from truth theory, set theory, cardinal arithmetic, and the theory of definability. We show that all four are instances of a single diagonal principle---\emph{Lawvere's fixed-point theorem}---and that, viewed through this principle, the classical paradoxes are precisely the statement that the negation map has no fixed point. We then explain why this obstruction is inevitable in classical logic (a nontrivial Boolean algebra admits no complement-fixed point) and how it is lifted in Belnap's four-valued logic, where the glut value \emph{Both} is a designated fixed point of negation. Assembling these facts yields a \emph{paradox dichotomy}: the diagonal endomap has no fixed point classically but a designated one paraconsistently, so the paradoxes become theorems exactly when classical logic is abandoned. Finally we exhibit an explicit finite paraconsistent model---a six-element theory over Belnap's values---in which the Liar, Russell, and Berry sentences are simultaneously provable and glut-valued, every provable sentence is designated (self-soundness), the principle of explosion fails by an explicit counterexample, and the inconsistency degree is exactly three.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Self-reference is the common thread running through the most famous paradoxes of logic and mathematics. The Liar sentence asserts its own falsity; Russell's set contains itself precisely when it does not; Cantor's diagonal set disagrees with every row of a purported enumeration; Berry's phrase names the number it claims to be too short to name. Historically these were studied in isolation, each spawning its own repair strategy---Tarski's hierarchy of languages for the Liar, the axiom of separation for Russell, the cumulative hierarchy for Cantor, and formal complexity theory for Berry.

The organizing observation of this paper is that the four arguments are one. The abstract statement that subsumes them is due to F.~W.~Lawvere: in any setting where an object can ``name its own functions'' (a point-surjection), every endomap of the codomain has a fixed point. Its contrapositive---a fixed-point-free endomap forbids self-naming---is the reusable diagonalization engine. Choosing the codomain and the fixed-point-free map recovers each paradox in turn.

Two conclusions follow. First, the classical paradoxes are \emph{impossibility theorems}: certain self-naming schemes cannot exist because negation (or complementation) has no fixed point. Second, the obstruction is \emph{logic-relative}: in a logic that furnishes a designated fixed point of negation, the very sentences classical logic rejects become provable. Belnap's four-valued logic is such a logic, and we build an explicit finite model witnessing that paradoxes and consistency coexist.

\section{Lawvere's fixed-point theorem}

\begin{definition}[Point-surjectivity]
Let $A$ and $C$ be types. A map $e : A \to (A \to C)$ is \textbf{point-surjective} if for every $f : A \to C$ there exists $a \in A$ with $e_a = f$, where $e_a := e(a)$. Each $a$ is a \emph{code} naming $e_a$, and point-surjectivity says every function $A \to C$ is named.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Lawvere]\label{thm:lawvere}
If $e : A \to (A \to C)$ is point-surjective, then every endomap $f : C \to C$ has a fixed point: there is $c \in C$ with $f(c) = c$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Given $f : C \to C$, consider $g : A \to C$ defined by $g(x) = f(e_x(x))$. By point-surjectivity there is $a$ with $e_a = g$. Evaluating at $a$,
\[
e_a(a) = g(a) = f(e_a(a)).
\]
Hence $c := e_a(a)$ satisfies $f(c) = c$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Contrapositive engine]\label{thm:contra}
If some $f : C \to C$ has no fixed point, then no $e : A \to (A \to C)$ is point-surjective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A point-surjective $e$ would, by Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere}, produce a fixed point of $f$, a contradiction.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Non-vacuity]
There exist types $A, C$ and a point-surjective $e : A \to (A \to C)$: take $A = C$ a singleton, and $e$ the unique map.
\end{proposition}

\section{The Liar, Cantor, and Russell as one diagonal}

The single fixed-point-free endomap powering the classical paradoxes is negation on propositions.

\begin{lemma}[The Liar]\label{lem:liar}
For every proposition $P$, $\neg(P \leftrightarrow \neg P)$. Equivalently $\neg P \neq P$: negation has no fixed point.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}
If $P \leftrightarrow \neg P$, then $P$ yields $\neg P$ and $\neg P$ yields $P$, a contradiction either way.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[No object enumerates its own predicates]\label{thm:noenum}
For every type $A$ and every $e : A \to (A \to \Prop)$, $e$ is not point-surjective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Apply Theorem~\ref{thm:contra} with $C = \Prop$ and $f = \neg$; by Lemma~\ref{lem:liar}, $\neg$ has no fixed point.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Cantor]
For every type $A$, no map $f : A \to \mathcal{P}(A)$ is surjective.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
A surjection $f$ induces $e_a(x) = [\,x \in f(a)\,]$ into $\Prop$; surjectivity of $f$ makes $e$ point-surjective (given predicate $p$, pick $a$ with $f(a) = \{x : p(x)\}$), contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:noenum}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Russell]
For any membership relation $\mathrm{mem} : A \to A \to \Prop$, there is no $r \in A$ with $\mathrm{mem}(x, r) \leftrightarrow \neg\,\mathrm{mem}(x, x)$ for all $x$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Instantiate $x := r$ to obtain $\mathrm{mem}(r,r) \leftrightarrow \neg\,\mathrm{mem}(r,r)$, contradicting Lemma~\ref{lem:liar}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Failure of naive comprehension]
For any $\mathrm{mem}$, not every predicate $p$ is realized as $\mathrm{mem}(\cdot, a)$; otherwise $a \mapsto \mathrm{mem}(\cdot, a)$ would be point-surjective into $\Prop$, contradicting Theorem~\ref{thm:noenum}.
\end{theorem}

\section{Berry's paradox: the counting shadow}

Fix an injective $\enc : \mathbb{N} \to \mathbb{N}$, thought of as assigning binary codewords.

\begin{definition}[Descriptive complexity]
For injective $\enc$, the \textbf{complexity} of $x$ is $K_{\enc}(x) = \size(\enc(x))$, the number of binary digits of $\enc(x)$.
\end{definition}

We use $\size(y) \le n \iff y < 2^n$.

\begin{theorem}[Finite Berry paradox]\label{thm:berry}
For injective $\enc$ and any $n$, at least one of $0, 1, \dots, 2^n$ has complexity strictly greater than $n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Otherwise $\enc(x) < 2^n$ for all $x \in \{0,\dots,2^n\}$, so $\enc$ injects a $(2^n+1)$-element set into a $2^n$-element set, impossible by pigeonhole.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Chaitin incompressibility]
For injective $\enc$, complexity is unbounded: for every $n$ there is $x$ with $K_{\enc}(x) > n$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:berry}. Injectivity is satisfiable (take $\enc = \mathrm{id}$), so the result is not vacuous.
\end{proof}

\section{The algebraic bridge and the paradox dichotomy}

\begin{theorem}[Boolean obstruction]\label{thm:boolobs}
In any Boolean algebra $\alpha$, if $x^c = x$ then $\bot = \top$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The complement laws give $x \wedge x^c = \bot$ and $x \vee x^c = \top$. Substituting $x^c = x$ and using idempotence, $x = \bot$ and $x = \top$, hence $\bot = \top$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
A nontrivial Boolean algebra has no negation fixed point: $x^c \neq x$ for all $x$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{definition}[Belnap four-valued logic]
The truth values are $\BV = \{\Ttt, \Fff, \Bbb, \Nnn\}$: \emph{True}, \emph{False}, \emph{Both} (glut), \emph{Neither} (gap). Negation is $\neg\Ttt = \Fff$, $\neg\Fff = \Ttt$, $\neg\Bbb = \Bbb$, $\neg\Nnn = \Nnn$. A value is \textbf{designated} iff it is $\Ttt$ or $\Bbb$.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}
Belnap negation is an involution: $\neg\neg v = v$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{theorem}[Designated fixed point]\label{thm:desfix}
There is a value $v$ with $\neg v = v$ and $v$ designated, namely $v = \Bbb$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proposition}[Glut characterization]
A value $v$ is a glut---both $v$ and $\neg v$ designated---iff $v = \Bbb$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Paradox dichotomy]\label{thm:dichotomy}
The diagonal endomap, negation, behaves oppositely on the two sides of the bridge:
\begin{enumerate}
\item In every nontrivial Boolean algebra, $x^c \neq x$: negation has no fixed point, so the Liar/Russell/Cantor diagonals are impossibility theorems.
\item In Belnap's logic there is a designated fixed point $\Bbb$: the same self-negating sentence becomes a provable glut.
\end{enumerate}
Consequently, the classical paradoxes become theorems precisely when classical logic is abandoned.
\end{theorem}

\section{A consistent paraconsistent witness model}

\begin{definition}[Paraconsistent theory]
A \textbf{paraconsistent theory} on a sentence type $S$ consists of $\mathrm{truth} : S \to \BV$ and $\mathrm{sentNeg} : S \to S$. A set $\mathrm{provable} \subseteq S$ is \textbf{sound} if every provable sentence is designated. The theory \textbf{has explosion} if from some glut ($\mathrm{truth}(p) = \Bbb$) every sentence is designated. Over finite $S$, the \textbf{inconsistency degree} is $\#\{s : \mathrm{truth}(s) = \Bbb\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{construction}[The six-element model]
Let $S = \{0,1,2,3,4,5\}$ with three distinguished tokens for the Liar, Russell, and Berry sentences. Assign value $\Bbb$ to those three (gluts), and ordinary values to the rest so that at least one sentence is valued $\Fff$. Take $\mathrm{provable}$ to be the designated sentences.
\end{construction}

\begin{theorem}[Paradoxes as theorems]
The three distinguished sentences are provable and glut-valued: each has value $\Bbb$, which is designated.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Self-soundness]
Every provable sentence is designated; the theory never asserts a merely-false sentence.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Non-explosion]
There is an explicit sentence valued $\Fff$, hence undesignated and unprovable. A contradiction is present, yet ``everything follows'' is not a theorem.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Bounded inconsistency]
The inconsistency degree is exactly three: precisely the Liar, Russell, and Berry sentences are gluts.
\end{theorem}

These four facts constitute an explicit certificate: a consistent, self-sound, non-explosive theory in which the Liar, Russell, and Berry sentences are provable and the contradictions are counted rather than uncontrolled.

\section{Applications and future work}

\textbf{Fault-tolerant reasoning.} Knowledge bases and web-scale corpora contain contradictory assertions; under classical explosion such systems become trivial. A Belnap-style semantics records a local contradiction as a glut and continues functioning, isolating rather than propagating inconsistency. Non-explosion is the formal guarantee.

\textbf{A uniform template.} Theorem~\ref{thm:contra} packages Cantor, Russell, and the Liar into one lemma: exhibit a fixed-point-free endomap of the codomain.

\textbf{Complexity theory.} The Berry/Chaitin bounds give the finite kernel of Kolmogorov--Chaitin incompressibility.

Future directions include: deriving G\"odel's diagonal lemma and first incompleteness theorem as Lawvere instances via a provability predicate and Gödel numbering; a category-theoretic restatement of Theorem~\ref{thm:lawvere} for cartesian closed categories; a full paraconsistent object language with a provability relation preserving non-explosion; and a quantitative Berry theorem giving the density of incompressible numbers.

\section{Conclusion}

A single diagonal---Lawvere's fixed-point theorem---underlies the Liar, Russell, Cantor, and Berry paradoxes. Classically they are impossibilities because negation has no fixed point; paraconsistently they are theorems because Belnap's glut \emph{Both} is a designated fixed point of negation. Theorem~\ref{thm:dichotomy} states this contrast precisely, and the six-element model demonstrates, by explicit computation, that a consistent, self-sound, non-explosive theory can prove all three paradox sentences at once.

